Z||000009||Songman||04-24-2001||11:28 PM||||I don't even know what a 93 Firebird dash looks like, but I know I want something cool and different in this car I am building. I'm definitely on the lookout. I imagine the problem with a dashboard out of these cars will be $$$$. My dash is already out so test fitting will not be a problem. Well, it is sitting in there, but not bolted down anyway...<P>If anyone has any links to pictures of these dashes, I'm interested.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000010||AusFiero||04-25-2001||06:09 AM||||Just to throw a bit of RHD drive conversion technology into this one. This is one way we do it when making new dashes. <P>Find the dash you like (don't worry too much about size but make sure the gauge clusters are what you like and are not too big for the car).<P>Next make a plug of the new dash in the car. Cutting up the old dash the gauges cames from makes this easier. Fill as necessary till you have the desired dash.<P>Take a fibreglass mould of the new shape and then make a dash.<P>To achieve a factory style finish we have restore a dash that coats any shape with a padded seamless vinyl finish. You probably have a similar service in the USA.<P>Install the new dash...bolt in the donor gauges etc and you have a factory type finish with no gaps or filler panels.<P>Just one way to do it anyway. Not the cheapest way but definately one of the neatest.||||reg||1
Z||000011||gabriel||04-25-2001||01:53 PM||||&gt;To achieve a factory style finish we have restore a dash that coats any shape with a padded seamless vinyl finish. You probably have a similar service in the USA.<P>  Okay, that got my interest.  Where can I get that service??  Anyone know?  Seems as though you could make any dash brand new ...||||reg||1
Z||000012||mrfixit58||04-25-2001||02:16 PM||||There's a place in California called "Just Dashes" that performs this service. If you decide to go that route, be prepared to write a BIG check.<P>I called them to inquire about recovering a 69 Firebird dash pad. I figures that since a re-pop pad was going to cost me $200 (and there're junk), for a little more I get could get an original that looked factory fresh. <P>Well... it wasn't a little more, it was a LOT more. I was quoted almost $400 plus shipping there and back!<P>Just be prepared,<BR>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000013||mrfixit58||04-25-2001||02:23 PM||||By the way, I wasn't trying to throw a wet blanket on the idea. I like it. As a matter of fact, I've been looking for just the right dash to do just what your talking about. Future projects... so many projects, so little time, so little $$$.<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000014||Songman||04-25-2001||06:19 PM||||I checked JustDashes too for dashpads for my 69 Firebird and my 69 Judge. If you want (and can afford) brand new, they are the way to go...<P>I am also interested in AusFiero's idea. Just don't know if I am technical enough to ake it work... I'd love to see pictures...<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
